## Proof

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains only the problem; no solution, hint, or answer is embedded. Proceeding.

Let $f:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ be a bijection satisfying
$$f(2x-f(x))=x,\qquad \forall x\in[0,1]. \tag{$\star$}$$

**Step 1. Rewrite as a relation between $f$ and $f^{-1}$.**

Since $f$ is bijective, applying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of $(\star)$ gives
$$2x-f(x)=f^{-1}(x),\qquad\text{i.e.,}\qquad f(x)+f^{-1}(x)=2x. \tag{1}$$

In particular $2x-f(x)=f^{-1}(x)\in[0,1]$, so the argument $2x-f(x)$ in $(\star)$ always lies in $[0,1]$, as required.

**Step 2. Derive a recurrence for the iterates of $f$.**

Substitute $x\mapsto f(x)$ in (1):
$$2f(x)-f(f(x))=f^{-1}(f(x))=x,$$
hence
$$f(f(x))=2f(x)-x. \tag{2}$$

Define the orbit $a_n:=f^{n}(x)$ for $n\in\mathbb Z$ (this is well-defined since $f$ is a bijection, so all iterates $f^{n}$, $n\in\mathbb Z$, exist). Equation (2) says
$$a_{n+1}=2a_n-a_{n-1}\qquad(n\ge 1),$$
and using $f^{-1}(x)=2x-f(x)=x-(f(x)-x)$ the same recurrence holds for $n\le 0$. Thus
$$a_{n+1}-a_n=a_n-a_{n-1}\quad\text{for all }n\in\mathbb Z,$$
so $(a_n)$ is an arithmetic progression. Writing $d:=f(x)-x=a_1-a_0$, we obtain
$$f^{n}(x)=a_n=x+nd,\qquad n\in\mathbb Z. \tag{3}$$

**Step 3. Force $d=0$.**

Every iterate $f^{n}(x)$ is a value of $f$ (or $f^{-1}$) on $[0,1]$, hence lies in $[0,1]$. So (3) gives
$$x+nd\in[0,1]\qquad\text{for all }n\in\mathbb Z.$$

If $d>0$, taking $n\to+\infty$ makes $x+nd\to+\infty$, eventually exceeding $1$ — contradiction. If $d<0$, taking $n\to+\infty$ makes $x+nd\to-\infty$, eventually below $0$ — contradiction. Therefore $d=0$.

**Step 4. Conclusion.**

Since $d=f(x)-x=0$ for every $x\in[0,1]$, we have $f(x)=x$ for all $x\in[0,1]$.

**Verification.** For $f(x)=x$: $f(2x-f(x))=f(2x-x)=f(x)=x$. ✓ Also $f$ is a bijection of $[0,1]$. ✓

Hence the unique bijection $f:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ satisfying $f(2x-f(x))=x$ is

$$\boxed{f(x)=x.}$$

